    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: `GOLD` membership tier for 3+ member households\n\nThe tier is a derived value computed in `OwnerMapper.membershipTier(owner)`. `GOLD` depends on the owner's **household size** (owners sharing the same `householdId`), which requires the repository \u2014 so I followed the existing pattern for repo-derived fields (the same way `bulkSignupWarning` is computed by the responder, not the mapper), keeping each piece of logic in one place.\n\n**Where each responsibility landed:**\n\n- **`Household.size(...)`** (`rest/function/owner/Household.java`) \u2014 the household is already defined here, so counting its members belongs here too. Counts owners sharing a non-null `householdId`; returns 1 for an owner in no shared household. A second overload counts within an already-loaded collection so a whole list/page is sized from one query.\n- **`Owner.householdSize`** \u2014 a `@Transient` (non-persisted) field carrying the derived size from the responding step to the mapper. No schema change.\n- **`OwnerMapper.membershipTier(...)`** \u2014 remains the single tier authority; now returns `GOLD` when `householdSize >= 3`, otherwise the unchanged `SILVER`/`BRONZE` rule.\n- **Responders** (`RespondWithOwner`, `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, `RespondWithOwnerUpdated`) \u2014 each sets `owner.setHouseholdSize(Household.size(owner, repo))` before mapping, one line alongside the existing `bulkSignupWarning` line.\n- **List endpoints** (`ListOwners`, `ListOwnersPage`) \u2014 size every returned owner from a single `findAll()`, so `GOLD` is consistent on list responses too (not just single-owner reads), without N+1 queries.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipTier` schema doc to describe the `GOLD` rule.\n\n**Results:** `GOLD` takes precedence over `SILVER`/`BRONZE`. The new `AcceptanceTest.coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold` passes (third household member reads `GOLD` via `GET`), and the full suite is `BUILD SUCCESS` with no regressions.",
